  "question": {
    "text": "The mean and standard deviation of 100 observations are 40 and 5.1 , respectively, By mistake one observation is taken as 50 instead of 40. If the correct mean and the correct standard deviation are $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ respectively, then $10(\\mu+\\sigma)$ is equal to"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "445",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "451",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "447",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "449",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned}<br/>& \\text { Actual means }=\\mu=\\frac{100(40)-50+40}{100} \\\\\n& \\mu=40-\\frac{1}{10}=39.9<br/>\\end{aligned}$<br/>Incorrect variance<br/>$\\begin{aligned} & (5.1)^2=\\frac{\\sum \\mathrm{x}_{\\mathrm{i}}^2}{100}-(\\overline{\\mathrm{x}})^2 \\\\ & \\sum \\mathrm{x}_{\\mathrm{i}}^2=100 \\times\\left(40^2\\right)+100(5.1)^2 \\\\ & \\sum \\mathrm{x}_{\\mathrm{i}}^2=16 \\times 10^4+(5.1)^2 \\times 100=162601 \\\\ & \\sigma^2=\\frac{\\sum \\mathrm{x}_{\\mathrm{i}}^2-50^2+40^2}{100}-(\\mu)^2 \\\\ & \\sigma^2=1617.01-(39.9)^2=25 \\\\ & \\sigma=5 \\\\ & 10(\\mu+\\sigma)=10(39.9+5) \\\\ & =10 \\times 44.9=449\\end{aligned}$"
  }
